proofs.proof_tex:
By the full-observation definition, there is a fixed Borel map \(T_n\) such that \(W_n=T_n(O_n^{\mathrm{obs}})\).  Thus every score test \(\psi_n\in\Psi_n\) induces the full-observation test \(\psi_n\circ T_n\in\Phi_n^{\mathrm{obs}}\), with the same rejection probability under every \(P\in\mathcal M_{2,\mathrm{loc}}\).  The score tests form a subclass of the full-observation tests, so the two minimax definitions imply
\[
 s_{n,\alpha,\beta}^{\mathrm{score}}
 \ge s_{n,\alpha,\beta}^{\mathrm{obs}}. \tag{1}
\]
The full-observable minimax theorem therefore yields, for every \(0<a\le2B\),
\[
 s_{n,\alpha,\beta}^{\mathrm{score}}
 \ge\frac{a^2(1-\alpha-\beta)}{\sqrt{2N_n}}. \tag{2}
\]
Its upper-bound witness is the unchanged test \(\phi_{n,\alpha}\), which is itself a common Borel function of \(W_n\).  The uniform size-and-separation theorem hence gives
\[
 s_{n,\alpha,\beta}^{\mathrm{score}}
 \le\rho_n(\alpha,\beta). \tag{3}
\]
The exact-unbiased proposition separately proves that
\(\mathbf 1\{\widehat\Omega_n^{\mathrm u}>q_n^{\mathrm u}(\alpha)\}\)
is a common Borel score-vector test with level at most \(\alpha\), power at least \(1-\beta\) whenever
\(\Omega_{\mathrm{pop}}\ge q_n^{\mathrm u}(\alpha)+q_n^{\mathrm u}(\beta)\), and no sample-centering bias term.  Finally, fix any \(a\in(0,2B]\).  Since \(N_n=n-2r\), the right side of (2) is bounded below by a positive constant times \(n^{-1/2}\), while \(\rho_n(\alpha,\beta)=O(n^{-1/2})\) for fixed \(r,B,\varepsilon,\alpha,\beta\).  Equations (2)--(3) establish the claimed \(\Theta(n^{-1/2})\) score-vector rate.
